  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 130 AM EDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nOnce the frontal boundary discussed in the short term clears through \nthe area on Sunday and into early next week, rain chances will lower \nback below 40-50% for most of the region as drier air arrives. \nStrong surface high pressure will re-develop in the wake of the \ntrough starting to advect offshore, creating a stout pressure \ngradient that could last several days into next week. Winds are \nfirst expected to increase on Sunday and last into the early to \nmiddle portion of next week, with several days of wind gusts in the \n20-30 mph range possible. \n\nAdditionally, with winds expected to remain northeasterly during \nthis time frame and stronger winds over the local waters, frictional \nconvergence along the coast is likely to be the main driver in rain \nshower or storm development for next week. Uncertainty in remains in \nwhat potential rainfall could amount to in this type of setup, but \nit is worth noting that we have seen these setups over-perform \nbefore. Therefore, we will continue to monitor trends closely. High \ntemperatures in the long term will fall to the low to mid 80s each \nday in the wake of the front late this weekend. Overnight lows will \nrange from the mid to upper 60s near the lake region to the low to \nmid 70s elsewhere.",
